<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-limits-of-polynomials" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Limits of Polynomials</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        4.0 Calculus
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        4.1 Functions
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title> 
                    <p>
                        Evaluate limits of polynomial functions in different situations
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>
                        11
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>
                        40 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <!-- [LP] Add guidance on activity -->

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Guidance on the Activity</title>
        <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>
                    10 minutes
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    Students observe how the outputs of a polynomial function behave as the input approaches a specific value and as it grows very large or very small.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Teacher's Role</title>
                <p>
                    Circulate and prompt students with questions such as "What do you notice about the outputs as <m>x</m> gets closer to <m>3</m>?" and "Does it matter which side you approach from?" Avoid giving away the answer about direct substitution, let students discover that pattern themselves. For the graph portion, draw attention to the direction the curve moves as <m>x</m> moves far to the left or far to the right.
                </p>
            </li>
        </dl>
    </axiom>

    <!-- Add Activity -->

        <activity xml:id="activity-exploring-polynomial-limits">
        <title>Exploring Limits of Polynomials</title>
        <p>
            Consider the polynomial function <m>f(x) = x^2 - 2x + 3</m>. In this activity, you will explore the behavior of the function as the input values approach a specific number and as the inputs become extremely large or extremely small.
        </p>
        <p>
            Begin by evaluating the function for values of <m>x</m> close to <m>3</m>. As you complete the table, try to predict each output before calculating it.
        </p>
        <p>
            <alert>
                Pay close attention to how the values of <m>f(x)</m> change as <m>x</m> approaches <m>3</m> from both the left and the right.
            </alert>
        </p>
        <table>
            <tabular top="major" bottom="major" left="medium" right="medium">
                <row>
                    <cell><m>x</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>2.9</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>2.99</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>3</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>3.01</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>3.1</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><m>f(x)</m></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>

        <p>
            After completing the table, discuss the following questions with a partner or as a group.
        </p>

        <p>
            <ol marker="(a)">
                <li>
                    <p>
                        What value do the outputs appear to approach as <m>x</m> gets closer to <m>3</m>?
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Do the values approaching from the left and from the right show the same pattern?
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Compare your observations with the exact value obtained by evaluating <m>f(3)</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        What does this suggest about the continuity of polynomial functions?
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </p>
        <p>
            Now look at the graph of <m>f(x) = x^2 - 2x + 3</m> below. Notice how the curve behaves as <m>x</m> moves far to the right and far to the left.
        </p>
        <figure xml:id="fig-activity-parabola">
            <caption>Graph of <m>f(x) = x^2 - 2x + 3</m></caption>
            <image width="60%" xml:id="img-activity-parabola">
                <latex-image>
                    \begin{tikzpicture}[&gt;=stealth, scale=1]
                        \draw[help lines, color=gray!30, dashed] (-2,-1) grid (5,12);
                        \draw[-&gt;, thick] (-2.5,0) -- (5.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
                        \draw[-&gt;, thick] (0,-1.2) -- (0,12.5) node[above] {$y$};
                        \foreach \x in {-2,-1,1,2,3,4,5}
                            \draw (\x,0.05) -- (\x,-0.05) node[below] {\small $\x$};
                        \foreach \y in {2,4,6,8,10,12}
                            \draw (0.05,\y) -- (-0.05,\y) node[left] {\small $\y$};
                        \draw[ultra thick, blue, &lt;-&gt;, domain=-1.3:4.3, samples=100]
                            plot (\x, {\x*\x - 2*\x + 3});
                        \filldraw[red] (3,6) circle (2pt) node[right] {\small $(3,6)$};
                    \end{tikzpicture}
                </latex-image>
            </image>
        </figure>
        <p>
            Use the graph to answer the following questions.
        </p>
        <p>
            <ol marker="(a)">
                <li>
                    <p>
                        What happens to the outputs as the magnitude of <m>x</m> increases without bound in the positive direction?
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Do the outputs appear to approach a fixed value, or do they continue to grow without bound?
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        How does the sign of <m>x</m> affect the outputs of the function for very large values?
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        What conclusion can you draw about the end behavior of polynomial functions from this graph?
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </p>
    </activity>


    <!-- [LP] Add guidance on structured lesson plan-->

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Structured Instruction</title>
        <p>
            <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>
                    15 minutes
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    To formalize the concepts and properties learners discovered in the activity.
                </p>
            </li>
            </dl>
        </p>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Key Takeaways and Teacher Connection</title>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> Ask students what they noticed when they substituted <m>x = 3</m> into the table. Guide them to see that the output matched what the table was approaching. This is the idea of direct substitution, and it works because polynomial functions have no breaks or holes.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <alert>Introducing Infinity:</alert> When discussing end behavior from the graph, introduce the symbol <m>\infty</m> as a way of describing outputs that grow without stopping in the positive direction and <m>-\infty</m> for outputs that decrease without stopping. Emphasize that these are not actual numbers but descriptions of unbounded behavior.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <alert>Leading Term Dominance:</alert> Ask students which term in <m>f(x) = x^2 - 2x + 3</m> they think matters most when <m>x</m> is very large. Help them see that <m>x^2</m> grows much faster than <m>2x</m> or <m>3</m>, so it controls the end behavior.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </paragraphs>
    </axiom>

    <!-- Add insight / key takeaway directly related to activity / learner experience -->

    <insight>
        <paragraphs xml:id="para-direct-substitution">
            <title>Limits of Polynomials at a Finite Point</title>
            <p>
                Polynomial functions are continuous everywhere, meaning they have no breaks, gaps, or sudden changes in value. Because of this, their limits at any real number can be found directly by substitution.
            </p>
            <p>
                If <m>P(x) = a_n x^n + a_{n-1} x^{n-1} + \dots + a_1 x + a_0</m> is a polynomial and <m>c</m> is a real number, then
                <md>\lim_{x \to c} P(x) = P(c)</md>
                so the limit is obtained by simply replacing <m>x</m> with <m>c</m>.
            </p>
        </paragraphs>

        <paragraphs xml:id="para-limits-at-infinity">
            <title>Limits of Polynomials at Infinity</title>
            <p>
                The symbol <m>\infty</m> is used to describe a quantity that grows larger and larger without ever stopping. It is not a number you can reach or calculate with; it simply means the outputs increase without bound. Similarly, <m>-\infty</m> means the outputs decrease without any lower bound. These symbols appear in limit notation whenever a function grows or falls indefinitely as the input becomes very large.
            </p>
            <p>
                To find limits as <m>x \to \infty</m> or <m>x \to -\infty</m>, we focus on the end behavior of the polynomial. For very large values of <m>x</m>, the highest power term dominates the expression, while all lower-degree terms become insignificant in comparison.
            </p>
            <p>
                The behavior of the polynomial is therefore determined by its leading term <m>a_n x^n</m>, and the limit can be found using
                <md>\lim_{x \to \infty} (a_n x^n + a_{n-1} x^{n-1} + \dots + a_0) = \lim_{x \to \infty} a_n x^n</md>
            </p>
            <p>
                For <m>x \to -\infty</m>, the same principle applies and the limit is also determined by the leading term alone:
                <md>\lim_{x \to -\infty} (a_n x^n + a_{n-1} x^{n-1} + \dots + a_0) = \lim_{x \to -\infty} a_n x^n</md>
            </p>
            <p>
                The final outcome depends on the degree of the polynomial and the sign of the leading coefficient. Even and odd powers behave differently as <m>x</m> becomes large in magnitude, and the sign of <m>a_n</m> determines whether the graph rises or falls.
            </p>
        </paragraphs>
    </insight>
<exploration xml:id="exploration-limit-approach">
    <title>Approaching a Limit</title>
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-limit-approach" platform="jsxgraph" width="90%%" aspect="16:9" source="jsxgraph/limit-approach-exploration.js">
        <slate xml:id="jsxgraph-limit-approach" surface="jsxboard" aspect="16:9"/>
        <instructions>
            <p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Click <c>Approach from Left</c>, then <c>Play</c>. Watch the point move toward <m>x = 3</m>. Does it move at a constant speed, or does it slow down? Why might that be a good way to show "approaching"?
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Watch the <em>Live Values</em> panel as the point moves. What happens to <m>|x - 3|</m>? What happens to the live conclusion message underneath it?
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Click <c>Reset</c>, then try <c>Approach from Right</c> and <c>Play</c> again. Does <m>f(x)</m> approach the same value, <m>6</m>, from this side too?
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Click <c>Free Drag</c> and drag the point yourself, back and forth across <m>x = 3</m>. Watch the point change color from red to orange to green. What does each color tell you about the distance from <m>x = 3</m>?
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Even though the point never lands exactly on <m>(3, 6)</m> during the animation, why can we still say <m>\lim_{x \to 3} (x^2 - 2x + 3) = 6</m>?
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>
        </instructions>
    </interactive>
</exploration>

<exploration xml:id="exploration-end-behavior">
    <title>End Behavior and the Leading Coefficient</title>
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-end-behavior" platform="jsxgraph" width="90%%" aspect="16:9" source="jsxgraph/end-behavior-exploration.js">
        <slate xml:id="jsxgraph-end-behavior" surface="jsxboard" aspect="16:9"/>
        <instructions>
            <p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            With <m>a = 1</m>, drag the <m>x</m> slider all the way to the left, then all the way to the right. What happens to <m>f(x)</m> in both directions?
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Now drag the <m>a</m> slider to a negative value, such as <m>a = -1</m>. Drag the <m>x</m> slider to the extremes again. What changed?
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Slide <m>a</m> very close to <m>0</m>. What does the graph turn into, and why does the description underneath change to mention a linear function?
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Based on everything you observed, complete this sentence in your own words: "The sign of the leading coefficient <m>a</m> tells you ______, regardless of the other terms in the polynomial."
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>
        </instructions>
    </interactive>
</exploration>

    <!-- Add further instruction (not in a block) to develop ideas as needed -->

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <!-- Add at least 3 examples -->

    <example xml:id="ex-drone-height">
        <statement>
            <p>
                A student is flying a small drone. The height of the drone is measured in meters, <m>t</m> seconds after takeoff is modeled by the polynomial function
            </p>

            <p>
                <md>h(t) = -2t^2 + 12t + 5</md>
            </p>

            <p>
                Determine the height that the drone approaches as the time gets closer and closer to <m>3</m> seconds. Express your answer using limit notation and evaluate the limit.
            </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
            <p>
                Begin by expressing the situation using limit notation.
            </p>

            <p>
                <md>\lim_{t \to 3} (-2t^2 + 12t + 5)</md>
            </p>

            <p>
                Since <m>h(t)</m> is a polynomial function, it is continuous for all values of <m>t</m>. Therefore, the limit can be found by substituting <m>t=3</m> directly into the function.
            </p>

            <p>
                Substitute and simplify.
            </p>

            <p>
                <md>
                    <mrow>h(3) \amp = -2(3)^2 + 12(3) + 5</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp = -2(9) + 36 + 5</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp = -18 + 36 + 5</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp = 23</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>

            <p>
                Therefore, as the time approaches <m>3</m> seconds, the height of the drone approaches <m>23</m> meters.
            </p>

            <p>
                <md>\lim_{t \to 3} (-2t^2 + 12t + 5)=23</md>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="ex-graph-end-behavior">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Look at the graph of the polynomial function <m>f(x) = x^3 - 3x</m> drawn below.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-cubic-graph-limit">
                <caption>Graph of <m>f(x) = x^3 - 3x</m></caption>
                <image width="60%" xml:id="img-cubic-limit">
                    <latex-image>
                        \begin{tikzpicture}[&gt;=stealth, scale=1]
                            \draw[help lines, color=gray!30, dashed] (-3,-4) grid (3,4);
                            \draw[-&gt;, thick] (-3.5,0) -- (3.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
                            \draw[-&gt;, thick] (0,-4.5) -- (0,4.5) node[above] {$y$};
                            \draw[ultra thick, blue, &lt;-&gt;, domain=-2.3:2.3, samples=100] plot (\x, {\x*\x*\x - 3*\x});
                        \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
            <p>
                Using the graph, find the following limits.
            </p>
            <p>
            <ol marker="(a)" cols="2">
                <li><m>\lim_{x \to \infty} f(x)</m></li>
                <li><m>\lim_{x \to -\infty} f(x)</m></li>
            </ol>
            </p>
            <p>
                After finding the answers from the graph, verify your results algebraically using the leading term of the function.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                We can find the answers by observing the visual trends on the graph.
            </p>
            <p>
            <ol marker="(a)">
                <li>
                    <p>
                        The limit as <m>x \to \infty</m> asks what happens to the <m>y</m>-values as we move far to the right. Looking at the right side of the graph, the blue curve points straight up. This means the values keep getting bigger without stopping. So, <m>\lim_{x \to \infty} f(x) = \infty</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        The limit as <m>x \to -\infty</m> asks what happens to the <m>y</m>-values as we move far to the left. Looking at the left side of the graph, the curve points straight down. This means the values keep getting smaller into the negatives. So, <m>\lim_{x \to -\infty} f(x) = -\infty</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="ex-bacteria-growth">
        <statement>
            <p>
                A biologist is studying how a new type of bacteria grows in a lab dish. She finds that the population <m>P</m> of the bacteria after <m>x</m> hours can be estimated by the function:
            </p>
            <p><md>P(x) = 0.5x^4 - 2x^3 + 10x + 50</md></p>
            <p>
                According to this math model, what happens to the bacteria population as time goes on forever? Evaluate <m>\lim_{x \to \infty} P(x)</m> and explain what the answer means in real life.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                To find out what happens as time goes on endlessly, we need to evaluate the limit as <m>x \to \infty</m>.
            </p>
            <p><md>\lim_{x \to \infty} (0.5x^4 - 2x^3 + 10x + 50)</md></p>
            <p>
                When dealing with infinity, only the term with the highest power matters. The leading term here is <m>0.5x^4</m>. All the other terms (<m>-2x^3</m>, <m>10x</m>, and <m>50</m>) grow so slowly in comparison that they become unnoticeable. We can therefore simplify the limit to just that leading term.
            </p>
            <p><md>\lim_{x \to \infty} 0.5x^4</md></p>
            <p>
                If you take a very large positive number and raise it to the power of <m>4</m>, it becomes a massive positive number. Multiplying it by <m>0.5</m> still leaves a massive positive number.
            </p>
            <p><md>\lim_{x \to \infty} 0.5x^4 = \infty</md></p>
            <p>
                <alert>What this means:</alert> According to this mathematical model, if the bacteria have enough food and space, the population will continue to grow larger and larger without any bound.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="ex-tricky-algebraic">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Evaluate the following limit algebraically.
            </p>
            <p><md>\lim_{x \to -\infty} (-3x^6 + 8x^5 - 2x^2 + 100)</md></p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                The leading term of this polynomial is <m>-3x^6</m>, so we reduce the limit to
            </p>
            <p><md>\lim_{x \to -\infty} -3x^6</md></p>
            <p>
                When <m>x</m> is a large negative number, raising it to the even power <m>6</m> produces a large positive result because even powers eliminate the negative sign. Multiplying that large positive value by the negative coefficient <m>-3</m> then produces a very large negative value. Therefore the limit decreases without bound.
            </p>
            <p>
                <md>\lim_{x \to -\infty} (-3x^6 + 8x^5 - 2x^2 + 100) = -\infty</md>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>


