    <!-- Add STACK exercises if made -->

    <!-- Add static exercises -->

    <exercises xml:id="exercises-polynomial-limits-practice">
        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the following limit using direct substitution.
                </p>

                <p>
                    <md>\lim_{x \to 4} (x^2 - 6x + 8)</md>
                </p>
            </statement>

            <answer>
                <p><m>0</m></p>
            </answer>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    Because the expression is a polynomial, the limit can be found by directly substituting <m>x = 4</m> into the function.
                </p>

                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\lim_{x \to 4} (x^2 - 6x + 8) \amp = (4)^2 - 6(4) + 8</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp = 16 - 24 + 8</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp = -8 + 8</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp = 0</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>

                <p>
                    Therefore,
                    <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to 4} (x^2 - 6x + 8) = 0</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A company's daily profit <m>P</m> (in dollars) based on selling <m>x</m> items is modeled by <m>P(x) = -0.1x^3 + 5x^2 + 100</m>. Evaluate <m>\lim_{x \to 10} P(x)</m> to find the profit when the items sold get very close to <m>10</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p><m>500</m> dollars</p>
            </answer>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    We substitute <m>x = 10</m> directly into the profit function to see what value it approaches.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\lim_{x \to 10} (-0.1x^3 + 5x^2 + 100) \amp = -0.1(10)^3 + 5(10)^2 + 100</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp = -0.1(1000) + 5(100) + 100</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp = -100 + 500 + 100 = 500</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Evaluate the following end behavior limits.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)" cols="2">
                        <li>
                            <md>\lim_{x \to \infty} (-4x^5 + 2x^2 - 1)</md>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <md>\lim_{x \to -\infty} (5x^4 - 3x^3 + x)</md>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>

            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><m>-\infty</m></li>
                        <li><m>\infty</m></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    To determine the end behavior of a polynomial, focus on the leading term because it dominates the expression for very large positive or negative values of <m>x</m>.
                </p>

                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                The leading term is <m>-4x^5</m>.
                            </p>

                            <p>
                                As <m>x \to \infty</m>, <m>x^5</m> becomes a very large positive number. Multiplying by the negative coefficient <m>-4</m> makes the expression decrease without bound.
                            </p>

                            <p>
                                <md>\lim_{x \to \infty} (-4x^5 + 2x^2 - 1)=\lim_{x \to \infty}(-4x^5)=-\infty</md>
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                The leading term is <m>5x^4</m>.
                            </p>

                            <p>
                                As <m>x \to -\infty</m>, a negative number raised to the even power <m>4</m> becomes positive. Multiplying by the positive coefficient <m>5</m> keeps the expression positive and increasing without bound.
                            </p>

                            <p>
                                <md>\lim_{x \to -\infty}(5x^4-3x^3+x)=\lim_{x \to -\infty}(5x^4)=\infty</md>
                            </p>
                        </li>

                    </ol>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Look at the polynomial <m>g(x) = ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + d</m>. If <m>a</m> is a negative number, determine the limit of <m>g(x)</m> as <m>x \to -\infty</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p><m>\infty</m></p>
            </answer>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    The leading term that controls the end behavior is <m>ax^3</m>. 
                </p>
                <p>
                    As <m>x \to -\infty</m>, the <m>x^3</m> portion will be a very large negative number because an odd power preserves the negative sign.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Since we are told the coefficient <m>a</m> is negative, we multiply a negative by a negative. This double negative cancels out, resulting in a positive infinity.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Suppose <m>P(x) = kx^2 - 3x + 2</m>, where <m>k</m> is an unknown constant. If <m>\lim_{x \to -2} P(x) = 20</m>, determine the value of <m>k</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p><m>3</m></p>
            </answer>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    Because this is a finite limit of a polynomial, we substitute <m>x = -2</m> directly into the expression and set it equal to <m>20</m>. From there, we solve the equation for <m>k</m>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>k(-2)^2 - 3(-2) + 2 \amp = 20</mrow>
                        <mrow>4k + 6 + 2 \amp = 20</mrow>
                        <mrow>4k + 8 \amp = 20</mrow>
                        <mrow>4k \amp = 12</mrow>
                        <mrow>k \amp = 3</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Consider the function <m>p(x) = \dfrac{x^2 - 9}{x - 3}</m>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Explain why <m>p(x)</m> is not defined at <m>x = 3</m> and describe what type of feature this creates on the graph.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Factor the numerator and simplify <m>p(x)</m> for all values of <m>x</m> other than <m>x = 3</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Use your simplified expression to evaluate <m>\lim_{x \to 3} p(x)</m> and explain why the limit exists even though <m>p(3)</m> is undefined.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Sketch a graph of <m>p(x)</m>, marking the location of the hole clearly on your diagram.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
                <p>
                    This example shows why limits are an important mathematical tool. Direct substitution fails at <m>x = 3</m>, yet we can still determine what value the function approaches at that point by using a limit. Limits allow us to describe the behavior of a function near a point even when the function itself is not defined there.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p><m>\lim_{x \to 3} p(x) = 6</m></p>
            </answer>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                At <m>x = 3</m> the denominator equals <m>3 - 3 = 0</m>, so division by zero makes <m>p(3)</m> undefined. On the graph, this creates a hole (also called a removable discontinuity) at the point where <m>x = 3</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                The numerator factors as <m>x^2 - 9 = (x+3)(x-3)</m>. For all <m>x \neq 3</m> we can cancel the common factor <m>(x-3)</m> to get
                                <md>p(x) = \frac{(x+3)(x-3)}{x-3} = x + 3</md>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Since <m>p(x) = x + 3</m> for all <m>x \neq 3</m>, the outputs of <m>p(x)</m> get closer and closer to <m>3 + 3 = 6</m> as <m>x</m> approaches <m>3</m>. Therefore
                                <md>\lim_{x \to 3} p(x) = 6</md>
                                The limit exists because the values of <m>p(x)</m> approach a single fixed number from both sides of <m>x = 3</m>, even though the function itself has no value there.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                The graph of <m>p(x)</m> looks exactly like the straight line <m>y = x + 3</m>, except that there is an open circle (a hole) at the point <m>(3, 6)</m> to show that this point is not included.
                            </p>
                            <figure xml:id="fig-hole-graph">
                                <caption>Graph of <m>p(x) = \dfrac{x^2-9}{x-3}</m> showing a hole at <m>(3,6)</m></caption>
                                <image width="60%" xml:id="img-hole-graph">
                                    <latex-image>
                                        \begin{tikzpicture}[&gt;=stealth, scale=1]
                                            \draw[help lines, color=gray!30, dashed] (-2,-1) grid (6,10);
                                            \draw[-&gt;, thick] (-2.5,0) -- (6.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
                                            \draw[-&gt;, thick] (0,-1.2) -- (0,10.5) node[above] {$y$};
                                            \foreach \x in {-2,-1,1,2,3,4,5,6}
                                                \draw (\x,0.05) -- (\x,-0.05) node[below] {\small $\x$};
                                            \foreach \y in {2,4,6,8,10}
                                                \draw (0.05,\y) -- (-0.05,\y) node[left] {\small $\y$};
                                            \draw[ultra thick, blue, &lt;-&gt;, domain=-1.5:5.5, samples=100]
                                                plot (\x, {\x + 3});
                                            \draw[fill=white, thick] (3,6) circle (3pt);
                                            \node[above right] at (3,6) {\small hole at $(3,6)$};
                                        \end{tikzpicture}
                                    </latex-image>
                                </image>
                            </figure>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>

    <!-- [LP] Add information on how to differentiate  -->

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Differentiation</title>

        <table>
            <title>Differentiation Strategies</title>
            <tabular halign="left">
                <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                    <cell>Learner Group</cell>
                    <cell>Strategy and Learning Experience</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell>
                    <cell>
                        Provide a completed row in the activity table as a worked example so students can see the pattern before completing the rest. Focus only on finite limits using direct substitution and delay end behavior until the idea of substitution is secure. Allow students to use a calculator to verify their outputs.
                    </cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell>
                    <cell>
                        The core lesson activities as described above.
                    </cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell>
                    <cell>
                        Direct advanced learners to the final exercise involving <m>p(x) = \dfrac{x^2 - 9}{x - 3}</m>. Ask them to investigate whether the limit and the function value are always the same thing, and to think of another example where a function is undefined at a point but the limit still exists.
                    </cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>
    </axiom>


